{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head 6188b53b787918eb29cbbf3a53febe85bfb73ce1, tree db5092a402e7e165e0eb0b5ccd86e719f426a89f, on builder/cb2-controls-20261116, all 7 artifacts, for request 2bc63ac8 under my promise 53700a84. This verdict names the one implementation: CB-2 (request ced2f264, promise 340635c1, report 57c8d160, the CB-2 note, primary). Main e3392a05 is the base. `git merge-tree --write-tree b0956c48 6188b53b` merges cleanly onto the current main, with tree 0bc85cba. This head answers my changes-requested verdict c2404ee4.\n\n## My three asks\n\n1. A refused plan no longer freezes the card. `reconcile()` returns `patched`, `rebuilt` or `refused`.\n   - If `plan()` throws for any reason, `rebuild()` runs. It refuses before any change when a live surface host would move into a new parent, or when the kept hosts would change order.\n   - Otherwise every live child that is not a kept host leaves through `retire()`, which ends its control state. Every wanted child enters fresh, and each kept host stays at its place in the root. Inserting before the next kept host keeps the wanted order.\n   - On `refused`, `render()` retires the controls, clears the root, and shows \"The card could not update. Read it again.\" with a \"Read the card again\" button. The surface host is detached there, and `SurfacePanels` still holds it, so the next render places it fresh through its placeholder. The notice therefore lasts only until the next refresh.\n   - The screen never keeps an earlier card while `display` holds a new one.\n2. The test. The bridge test's new case swaps two keyed actions in one card identity. It asserts `rebuilt`, the new order and the surface host in place. A click on the earlier control sends nothing, because it is detached and its state is retired. A click on the new control runs the action it shows. A tree that nests the live host in a new parent returns `refused`, and the host stays. That is my \"runs the action it shows\" branch. After a rebuild every control is new, so that branch is the right one.\n   - The paired control, `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-17-cb2-gate/control-reorder-without-fallback.log`, fails that case with \"The Card changed the order of retained controls.\" and passes the other 11.\n3. The note now describes the rebuild and the notice, and no longer calls the throw safe.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- The diff from cd086606 to this head: `reconcile.ts` (`reconcile`, `rebuild`), `main.ts` `render()`, the test, the note and the regenerated `generated.ts`. I reviewed the rest of CB-2 in c2404ee4, and none of it changed.\n- The gate, END b23607b5. `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-17-cb2-gate/gate.log` names head 6188b53b and tree db5092a4 with 0 changed files. Plain 4996 with 1 skipped passed, one more case than before: the reorder case. Native 1153 with 2 skipped passed, with the scale tier omitted as outside-scale-paths, which I accepted in c2404ee4. Lost crossings were 0, and the log ends \"gate passed\".\n- The gate ran after the planner's END bf9e88fd closed the AR-1 rate read. No compile ran beside the deployment.\n- Scope. The 7 artifacts name the head, and their paths equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only e3392a05 6188b53b`.\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 6 checked, 0 flagged. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, and `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The first choice is a patch in place, which keeps a held press alive. The fallback is a full rebuild, which loses only the press in flight, as before CB-2. A terminal notice replaces the card when a host cannot stay. No path leaves stale content on screen.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The gesture check and the captured cut from c2404ee4 stand. A rebuild makes new controls bound to their own actions, and a retired control sends nothing.\n\nSimplification: no required change. `rebuild()`'s `live` helper and the placeholder rule duplicate a little of `fresh()`. That is acceptable for a fallback path.\n\nThe note's limits stand: a press during a background read, and LW-1. This approval authorizes no push, landing or deployment. The planner decides the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:57c8d160415b4403ade0a961e1fbd3dd9b7c1ff5","binding":"assigned","head":"6188b53b787918eb29cbbf3a53febe85bfb73ce1","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:53700a842d5a0c69bc299a0884d89ddd462990a6\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:340635c1acf9bc9cb3657de587cd5a8679d24934\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:53700a842d5a0c69bc299a0884d89ddd462990a6","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}